      "question": {
        "math_academy_id": "q-49387",
        "knowledge_point_id": "2feb2115-bd75-5c09-87cc-f10cf46c182f",
        "knowledge_point": "Calculating Indefinite Integrals of Algebraic Functions Using Substitution",
        "problem": "Given that $u=1+{x}^{2}$, which integral is equivalent to $\\int \\frac{{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x}{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}}}?$",
        "worked_solution": "Since $u=1+{x}^{2}$, then we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2x\\,⟹\\,x\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe also note that\n${x}^{2}=u-1$\n\nNow we can rewrite the integral in terms of $u:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x}{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}}} & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}}}\\cdot {x}^{2}\\cdot x\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot (u-1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int ({u}^{1/2}-{u}^{-1/2})\\text{d}u\\end{aligned}\n$$",
        "answer_fields": [
          {
            "key": "selection",
            "type": "radio",
            "choices_complete": true,
            "choices": [
              {
                "type": "math",
                "value": "\\frac{1}{2}\\int {u}^{-1/2}\\,\\text{d}u",
                "option": "0",
                "source_option": "a"
              },
              {
                "type": "math",
                "value": "\\frac{1}{2}\\int ({u}^{1/2}-{u}^{-1/2})\\text{d}u",
                "option": "1",
                "source_option": "b"
