        "math_academy_id": "q-49532",
        "knowledge_point_id": "94ad8efa-33ee-5451-b6b9-c99cb81a9ba2",
        "knowledge_point": "Calculating Definite Integrals of Algebraic Functions Using Substitution",
        "problem": "Given that $u=3-2{x}^{2}$, which integral is equivalent to ${\\int }_{-1}^{0}{x}^{3}\\sqrt{3-2{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x?$",
        "worked_solution": "Since $u=3-2{x}^{2}$, then we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-4x\\,⟹\\,-\\frac{\\text{d}u}{4}=x\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe also note that ${x}^{2}=\\frac{1}{2}(3-u).$ \n\nComputing our limits of integration, we get\n\n| **$x$** | $-1$ | $0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **$u$** | $1$ | $3$ |\n\nWe can now solve the integral using our new variable $u,$ remembering to use the new limits of integration:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{x}^{3}\\sqrt{3-2{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{x}^{2}\\sqrt{3-2{x}^{2}}\\cdot x\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{1}{2}(3-u)\\sqrt{u}\\cdot (-\\frac{\\text{d}u}{4}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}{\\int }_{1}^{3}(3-u)\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u\\end{aligned}\n$$",
