    {
      "directory": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067661",
      "id": "q-116621",
      "status": "supported",
      "reason": null,
      "question": {
        "math_academy_id": "q-116621",
        "knowledge_point_id": "903cd6d7-18f2-5b53-a8e9-9aaef5589549",
        "knowledge_point": "Using the Trapezoidal Rule: Word Problem",
        "problem": "The following table shows the number of customers per minute, $c(t),$ that arrive at a shop counter over a period of $120$ minutes.\n\n| $t\\,(\\text{minutes})$ | $0$ | $30$ | $80$ | $120$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $c(t)\\,(\\text{customers/minute})$ | $10$ | $8$ | $6$ | $12$ |\n\nUse the trapezoidal rule with the partition\n\n$$\n[0,30]\\cup [30,80]\\cup [80,120],\n$$\n\nto approximate the total number of customers served in two hours.",
        "worked_solution": "In this context, we have $3$ trapezoids, and the area $A$ under the curve $y=c(t)$ represents the total number of customers served in $2$ hours.\n\nUsing the trapezoidal rule, we estimate the area as follows:\n\n$$\nA≈\\frac{c(0)+c(30)}{2}\\cdot Δ{t}_{1}+\\frac{c(30)+c(80)}{2}\\cdot Δ{t}_{2}+\\frac{c(80)+c(120)}{2}\\cdot Δ{t}_{3}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the appropriate numbers into the above expression gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈\\frac{10+8}{2}\\cdot (30-0)+\\frac{8+6}{2}\\cdot (80-30)+\\frac{6+12}{2}\\cdot (120-80) \\\\  & =9\\cdot 30+7\\cdot 50+9\\cdot 40 \\\\  & =980\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total number of customers serve approximately is $980$ in $2$ hours.",
        "answer_fields": [
          {
            "key": "selection",
            "type": "radio",
            "choices_complete": true,
            "choices": [
