        "knowledge_point": "Rewriting One of the Limits of an Improper Integral",
        "problem": "Which of the following is equivalent to \n${\\int }_{-6}^{-3}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-9}}?$",
        "worked_solution": "This is an improper integral of the second kind because the function under the integral is unbounded as we approach the *upper* integration limit from any point that lies in the interval $x\\in (-6,-3):$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-9}}\\to \\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to (-3)^{-}\n$$\n\nWe can attempt to evaluate this integral by integrating $f(x)$ over the interval $x\\in [-6,b]$ and taking the limit as $b\\to (-3)^{-}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-6}^{-3}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-9}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{b\\to (-3)^{-}}({\\int }_{-6}^{b}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-9}}\\,\\text{d}x)\\end{aligned}\n$$",
