        "knowledge_point": "Rewriting One of the Limits of an Improper Integral",
        "problem": "Which of the following is equivalent to \n${\\int }_{-1}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{4-{x}^{2}}?$",
        "worked_solution": "This is an improper integral of the second kind because the function under the integral is unbounded as we approach the *upper* integration limit from any point that lies in the interval $x\\in (-1,2):$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{4-{x}^{2}}\\to \\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to {2}^{-}\n$$\n\nWe can attempt to evaluate this integral by integrating $f(x)$ over the interval $x\\in [-1,b]$ and taking the limit as $b\\to {2}^{-}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{4-{x}^{2}} & ={lim}_{b\\to {2}^{-}}({\\int }_{-1}^{b}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{4-{x}^{2}})\\end{aligned}\n$$",
