        "knowledge_point": "The Shortest Distance Between a Point and a Perpendicular Bisector",
        "problem": "Use calculus to find the closest point to $P(1,2)$ that is equidistant to the points $A(-2,0)$ and $B(1,3).$",
        "worked_solution": "Let's sketch the situation.\n\n \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068082/assets/052b405c104f0d6bc353782c6e07bfb1e0245726d86346a545ea38aa31941711.png)\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The dotted line is the *perpendicular bisector* of the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$\n\n- All the points on this perpendicular bisector are equidistant to $A$ and $B.$\n\n- Our task is to find the point $Q$ on this line that's closest to $P.$\n\nFirst, we create a function $S$ which represents the *squared* distance between the point $P(1,2)$ and a general point $Q(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x,y) & =(x-1)^{2}+(y-2)^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2x-4y+5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe required point $Q(x,y)$ is equidistant to the points $A(-2,0)$ and $B(1,3).$ So, by writing down $(QA)^{2}=(QB)^{2},$ we have the following constraint equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{(x-(-2))^{2}+(y-0)^{2}}_{⏟}}_{(QA)^{2}} & ={{(x-1)^{2}+(y-3)^{2}}_{⏟}}_{(QB)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe make $y$ the subject in the constraint equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+2)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =(x-1)^{2}+(y-3)^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+4x+4 & ={x}^{2}-2x+1+{y}^{2}-6y+9 \\\\ \\cancel{{x}^{2}}+\\cancel{{y}^{2}}+4x+4 & =\\cancel{{x}^{2}}-2x+1+\\cancel{{y}^{2}}-6y+9 \\\\ 6y & =-6x+10-4 \\\\ y & =-x+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our expression for $S(x,y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x) & ={x}^{2}+(-x+1)^{2}-2x-4(-x+1)+5 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{x}^{2}-2x+1-2x+4x-4+5 \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the first derivative,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x) & =4x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solve ${S}^{′}(x)=0$ to find the stationary point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 4x & =0 \\\\ x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's verify that this stationary point is a minimum. Taking the second derivative, we find\n\n$$\n{S}^{″}(0)=4>0.\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the stationary point $x=0$ indeed minimizes the squared distance. \n\nFinally, the $y$-coordinate is\n\n$$\ny=-0+1=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required point is $(0,1).$",
