        "problem": "Use calculus to find the closest point to $P(6,7)$ that is equidistant to the points $A(0,1)$ and $B(0,6).$",
        "worked_solution": "Let's sketch the situation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068082/assets/c4fd419e12879af4910e5d8364426c14342c3dadb970d8bbc43d31213a6ff947.png)\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The dotted line is the *perpendicular bisector* of the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$\n\n- All the points on this perpendicular bisector are equidistant to $A$ and $B.$\n\n- Our task is to find the point $Q$ on this line that's closest to $P.$\n\nFirst, we create a function $S$ which represents the *squared* distance between the point $P(6,7)$ and a general point $Q(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x,y) & =(x-6)^{2}+(y-7)^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-12x-14y+85\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe required point $Q(x,y)$ is equidistant to the points $A(0,1)$ and $B(0,6).$ So, by writing down $(QA)^{2}=(QB)^{2},$ we have the following constraint equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{(x-0)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}}_{⏟}}_{(QA)^{2}} & ={{(x-0)^{2}+(y-6)^{2}}_{⏟}}_{(QB)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe make $y$ the subject in the constraint equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-0)^{2}+(y-1)^{2} & =(x-0)^{2}+(y-6)^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2y+1 & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-12y+36 \\\\ \\cancel{{x}^{2}}+\\cancel{{y}^{2}}-2y+1 & =\\cancel{{x}^{2}}+\\cancel{{y}^{2}}-12y+36 \\\\ 10y & =35 \\\\ y & =\\frac{7}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our expression for $S(x,y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x) & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-12x-14y+85 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-12x-14(\\frac{7}{2})+85 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-12x+\\frac{193}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the first derivative,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x) & =2x-12,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solve ${S}^{′}(x)=0$ to find the stationary point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 2x-12 & =0 \\\\ x & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's verify that this stationary point is a minimum. Taking the second derivative, we find\n\n$$\n{S}^{″}(6)=2>0.\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the stationary point $x=6$ indeed minimizes the squared distance. \n\nFinally, the $y$-coordinate is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{7}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required point is $(6,\\frac{7}{2}).$",
