      "question": {
        "math_academy_id": "q-118389",
        "knowledge_point_id": "38bfb48e-9b66-58a8-966a-fb6938e08073",
        "knowledge_point": "Finding an Equation That Determines a Minimum Distance",
        "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068172/assets/37434a61262f829160fc2b19fcadb67745a024d589cd1ce318ca4ab90d6ab2d9.png)\n\nThe $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ on the curve $y=\\frac{x+1}{\\sqrt{x}}$ that is closest to the point $(2,0)$ satisfies the equation\n\n$$\n2x-3-=0.\n$$\n\n \nWhat is the missing expression?",
        "worked_solution": "Let's consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(2,0)$ and a general point $P(x,y):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x,y) & =(x-2)^{2}+(y-0)^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-4x+4+{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y=\\frac{x+1}{\\sqrt{x}}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={x}^{2}-4x+4+{y}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-4x+4+{(\\frac{x+1}{\\sqrt{x}})}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-4x+4+\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{x} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-4x+4+\\frac{{x}^{2}+2x+1}{x} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-4x+4+x+2+\\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-3x+\\frac{1}{x}+6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(x)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x)=2x-3-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$",
